    98	## MCP schema breaking — worked examples
    99	
   100	The MCP tool catalog is a structured contract: callers depend on tool names,
   101	required input args, accepted input enum values, output property names, and
   102	output enum values. The `scripts/mcp-schema-breaking.sh` detector classifies
   103	each diff against the base snapshot into one of four breaking shapes plus
   104	two non-breaking shapes. The table below pins down how to bump on each.
   105	
   106	| Shape | Example | Breaking? | Required label | Required bump |
   107	|---|---|---|---|---|
   108	| Tool removal / rename | `cf_evaluate` removed from `mcp_tools.names` | yes | `mcp-schema-breaking` | major (cliff after 2-major visibility) |
   109	| Required-arg added | `spider.required` gains `timeout_ms` while `url` is kept | yes | `mcp-schema-breaking` | major |
   110	| Input enum narrowed | `vpn_rotate.strategy` drops the `random` value | yes | `mcp-schema-breaking` | major |
   111	| Output property removed | `recipe_show.output.properties` drops `endpoints` | yes | `mcp-schema-breaking` | major |
   112	| Output enum narrowed | `recipe_show.output.enums.status` drops `Pending` | yes | `mcp-schema-breaking` | major |
   113	| New tool added | new entry in `mcp_tools.names` | no | `api-additive` | minor |
   114	| Required-arg relaxed | a previously required arg becomes optional | no | `api-additive` | minor |
   115	
   116	### Case 1 — Enum narrowing (output): `recipe_show.status` loses `Pending`
   117	
   118	```diff
   119	- "enums": { "status": ["Active", "Pending", "Retired"] }
   120	+ "enums": { "status": ["Active", "Retired"] }
   121	```
   122	
   123	A consumer that previously did `match status { Pending => … }` now silently
   124	loses the branch. This is **major-bump breaking**, not minor — even though
   125	the response payload still parses. Label: `mcp-schema-breaking`. The
   126	detector emits the line:
   127	
   128	```
   129	[mcp-schema-breaking] BREAKING vs <base>: output enum narrowed
   130	    - recipe_show.status: removed "Pending"
   131	```
   132	
   133	### Case 2 — Required field added (input): keep `url`, add new required `timeout_ms`
   134	
   135	```diff
   136	- "required": ["url"]
   137	+ "required": ["timeout_ms", "url"]
   138	```
   139	
   140	Old callers that omit `timeout_ms` will be rejected by the input-schema
   141	validator, so this is **major-bump breaking**. Label:
   142	`mcp-schema-breaking`. The detector emits:
   143	
   144	```
   145	[mcp-schema-breaking] BREAKING vs <base>: tightened required-args
   146	    - spider.timeout_ms
   147	```
   148	
   149	If instead the new field were *optional*, the change would be additive —
   150	`api-additive` + minor bump.
   151	
   152	### Case 3 — Output type changed: `auth_status.status` widens from a free-form string to a closed enum
   153	
   154	```diff
   155	  "properties": {
   156	    "status": {
   157	-     "type": "string"
   158	+     "type": "string",
   159	+     "enum": ["Valid", "ExpiringSoon", "ExpiringCritical",
   160	+              "PartiallyExpired", "AllExpired", "Missing"]
   161	    }
   162	  }
   163	```
   164	
   165	This is **subtle**: narrowing the *type* (from any-string to a closed enum)
   166	locks future server-side additions to a follow-up bump, but existing callers
   167	that already only emit valid values are unaffected. The R2 detector flags
   168	this as an **output enum narrowing** vs *no enum at all* in the base
   169	snapshot — but the relevant `mcp_tools.schemas.auth_status.output.enums`
   170	entry **did not exist** in the base snapshot, so the narrowing detector
   171	does not fire. To make this break the gate, the snapshot must already
   172	carry the prior enum literal; if it doesn't (i.e. the field was previously
   173	documented as a free-form string), promote this PR to
   174	`mcp-schema-breaking` manually and bump major. Rule of thumb: any
   175	*type-shape* change is a major bump even when the detector cannot prove
   176	it from the snapshot diff alone.
   177	
   178	## Where to look next

